class PartitiveRelation {
  definition {
    ISO 704 / ISO 1087-1 / ISO 12620 partitive relation.

    Connects a comprehensive concept (superordinate concept
    partitive) to two or more partitive concepts (subordinate
    concepts partitive) which fitted together constitute the
    comprehensive concept.

    Shown as a rake or bracket in source diagrams. All partitives
    within one relation are coordinate concepts (ISO 12620): they
    share the comprehensive AND share the criterion of subdivision.

    Distinct from RelatedConcept binary edges has_part / is_part_of,
    which express pairwise part-of assertions without completeness
    or presence/count claims. A single partitive should be expressed
    as a binary edge, not a PartitiveRelation (ISO requires two or
    more partitives).

    Mirrors AbstractNaryRelation shape. Field `comprehensive` denotes
    the whole concept (superordinate concept partitive).

    Per-file storage: lives at
    `relations/<comprehensive-id>/<criterion-slug>.yaml`. See
    docs/design/relations-as-files.md.

    ISO 704:2022 per-member features (MECE decomposition):
      - presence (required | optional)
      - count (exactly_one | at_least_one | multiple)
      - is_delimiting (delimiting part — bold 3x-width line)

    Wire field `members` is uniform across all n-ary relation types
    (was: `partitives` — renamed in the v3.3 clean break).
  }
  +comprehensive: <<StandardDocument>> ReferenceToTermbase [1] {
    definition {
      The superordinate concept partitive — the whole that the
      partitives fitted together constitute.
    }
  }
  +members: PartitiveMember [2..*] {
    definition {
      The subordinate concepts partitive — the parts. Each member
      carries a ConceptRef plus ISO 704:2022 presence, count, and
      delimiting metadata.

      ISO 704 requires "two or more." A single partitive should be
      expressed as a binary has_part edge instead.
    }
  }
  +completeness: Completeness [0..1] {
    definition {
      Whether the encoded partitives fitted together constitute the
      comprehensive (complete) or only some of it (partial — other
      partitives exist that are not encoded).

      Defaults to complete when omitted.
    }
  }
  +criterion: <<BasicDocument>> LocalizedString [0..1] {
    definition {
      The principle by which the comprehensive is divided into
      partitives. ISO 12620 coordinate-concept coherence: partitives
      within one relation share the comprehensive AND the criterion.

      Two PartitiveRelations on the same comprehensive with the same
      criterion are duplicates (validation error). Two with different
      criteria are distinct decompositions.

      Multilingual (relation-level metadata).
    }
  }
  +sources: ConceptSource [0..*] {
    definition {
      Bibliographic references for this specific partitive relation.
      Independent of the comprehensive concept's own sources.
    }
  }
  +notes: <<BasicDocument>> LocalizedString [0..1] {
    definition {
      Free-form localized commentary on this relation.
    }
  }
  +status: ConceptStatus [0..1] {
    definition {
      Lifecycle status of this relation, independent of the
      comprehensive concept's status.
    }
  }
}
